Task 1: Explore the Architecture of IdM Components
Subtask 1.1: Understanding IdM Architecture Overview
Identity Management (IdM) is built on four core components that work together to provide comprehensive identity services:

LDAP (Lightweight Directory Access Protocol): Stores user and group information
Kerberos: Provides secure authentication services
DNS (Domain Name System): Enables service discovery and name resolution
PKI (Public Key Infrastructure): Manages digital certificates and encryption
Let's start by examining the overall IdM installation and its components.

# Check IdM server status
sudo systemctl status ipa

# View IdM server information
ipa config-show
Subtask 1.2: Examining LDAP Component
LDAP serves as the central directory store for all identity information in IdM.

# Check LDAP service status
sudo systemctl status dirsrv@YOUR-REALM-NAME

# View LDAP directory structure
ipa config-show | grep "IPA masters"

# Explore LDAP base DN (Distinguished Name)
ldapsearch -x -H ldap://localhost -b "dc=example,dc=com" -s base
Note: Replace YOUR-REALM-NAME with your actual realm name, and dc=example,dc=com with your actual base DN.

Subtask 1.3: Examining Kerberos Component
Kerberos provides secure authentication through tickets and encryption.

# Check Kerberos KDC (Key Distribution Center) status
sudo systemctl status krb5kdc

# View Kerberos configuration
cat /etc/krb5.conf

# Check Kerberos database
sudo kadmin.local -q "listprincs"
Subtask 1.4: Examining DNS Component
DNS in IdM provides service location and name resolution for the domain.

# Check DNS service status
sudo systemctl status named-pkcs11

# View DNS zones managed by IdM
ipa dnszone-find

# Check DNS records for your domain
dig @localhost your-domain.com ANY
Subtask 1.5: Examining PKI Component
PKI manages certificates and provides encryption services.

# Check Certificate Authority status
sudo systemctl status pki-tomcatd@pki-tomcat

# View CA information
ipa ca-find

# Check certificate store
certutil -L -d /etc/pki/nssdb
Task 2: Use IPA Commands to Interact with Each Service
Subtask 2.1: Interacting with LDAP through IPA Commands
LDAP operations in IdM are performed through ipa commands that abstract the complexity of direct LDAP queries.

Creating and Managing Users (LDAP Operations)
# Authenticate as admin
kinit admin

# Create a new user (stored in LDAP)
ipa user-add testuser1 --first=Test --last=User --email=testuser1@example.com

# View user information from LDAP
ipa user-show testuser1

# List all users in LDAP directory
ipa user-find

# Modify user attributes in LDAP
ipa user-mod testuser1 --title="Software Engineer"

# Search for users with specific criteria
ipa user-find --first=Test
Managing Groups (LDAP Operations)
# Create a group (stored in LDAP)
ipa group-add developers --desc="Development Team"

# Add user to group
ipa group-add-member developers --users=testuser1

# View group information
ipa group-show developers

# List all groups
ipa group-find
Subtask 2.2: Interacting with Kerberos through IPA Commands
Kerberos authentication is integrated into IdM and managed through ipa commands.

Managing Kerberos Principals
# View Kerberos realm information
ipa config-show | grep realm

# Set user password (creates Kerberos principal)
ipa passwd testuser1

# View service principals
ipa service-find

# Create a service principal
ipa service-add HTTP/webserver.example.com

# View host principals
ipa host-find
Testing Kerberos Authentication
# Test Kerberos authentication for the new user
# First, destroy current tickets
kdestroy

# Authenticate as the new user
kinit testuser1

# View current Kerberos tickets
klist

# Test ticket renewal
kinit -R

# Return to admin authentication
kinit admin
Subtask 2.3: Interacting with DNS through IPA Commands
IdM's integrated DNS can be managed through ipa commands for service discovery and name resolution.

Managing DNS Zones and Records
# View existing DNS zones
ipa dnszone-find

# Add a DNS record
ipa dnsrecord-add example.com webserver --a-rec=192.168.1.100

# View DNS records for a zone
ipa dnsrecord-find example.com

# Add a CNAME record
ipa dnsrecord-add example.com www --cname-rec=webserver.example.com.

# Test DNS resolution
nslookup webserver.example.com localhost
Managing DNS Configuration
# View DNS configuration
ipa dnsconfig-show

# View DNS servers
ipa dnsserver-find

# Check DNS forwarders
ipa dnsconfig-show | grep forwarder
Subtask 2.4: Interacting with PKI through IPA Commands
PKI services in IdM manage certificates and provide encryption capabilities.

Managing Certificates
# View Certificate Authority information
ipa ca-show ipa

# Request a certificate for a service
ipa cert-request certificate_request.csr --principal=HTTP/webserver.example.com

# View certificates
ipa cert-find

# Check certificate status
ipa cert-show 1
Managing Certificate Profiles
# List certificate profiles
ipa certprofile-find

# View a specific certificate profile
ipa certprofile-show caIPAserviceCert

# List certificate authorities
ipa ca-find
Task 3: Analyzing Component Integration
Subtask 3.1: Understanding Service Dependencies
Let's examine how the components work together:

# Check all IdM-related services
sudo systemctl list-units --type=service | grep -E "(ipa|krb5|dirsrv|named|pki)"

# View service dependencies
sudo systemctl list-dependencies ipa

# Check IdM topology
ipa topologysegment-find domain
ipa topologysegment-find ca
Subtask 3.2: Testing Integrated Authentication Flow
# Test complete authentication flow
# 1. DNS resolution
nslookup ipa.example.com

# 2. Kerberos authentication
kinit testuser1

# 3. LDAP query with Kerberos authentication
ipa user-show testuser1

# 4. Certificate-based operations
ipa cert-find --user=testuser1
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Service Not Running
# Check service status
sudo systemctl status service-name

# View service logs
sudo journalctl -u service-name -f

# Restart service if needed
sudo systemctl restart service-name
Issue 2: Authentication Problems
# Clear Kerberos cache
kdestroy

# Check Kerberos configuration
cat /etc/krb5.conf

# Test basic authentication
kinit admin
Issue 3: DNS Resolution Issues
# Test DNS resolution
nslookup your-domain.com localhost

# Check DNS configuration
ipa dnsconfig-show

# Verify DNS service
sudo systemctl status named-pkcs11
Verification and Testing
Comprehensive System Test
# Create a test script to verify all components
cat > test_idm_components.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "=== Testing IdM Components ==="

echo "1. Testing DNS..."
nslookup $(hostname) localhost && echo "DNS: OK" || echo "DNS: FAILED"

echo "2. Testing Kerberos..."
kinit admin && echo "Kerberos: OK" || echo "Kerberos: FAILED"

echo "3. Testing LDAP..."
ipa user-find admin > /dev/null && echo "LDAP: OK" || echo "LDAP: FAILED"

echo "4. Testing PKI..."
ipa ca-find > /dev/null && echo "PKI: OK" || echo "PKI: FAILED"

echo "=== Test Complete ==="
EOF

chmod +x test_idm_components.sh
./test_idm_components.sh
